{
  "topic": "types of fractions",
  "affirmation": "You brought fractions to life with clear models and warm real examples.",
  "identity": "You teach by showing first — you put a shape on the board, counted its parts with students, and then tied that same idea to everyday life like cake so the idea landed in children's everyday language. Your voice is patient and encouraging; you welcome many volunteers and gently fix confusions in the moment.",
  "moments": [
    {
      "title": "Partition question",
      "quote": "کتنے پارٹس بنیں گے آپ کے حساب سے؟",
      "why": "You used a simple, visual prompt to move the class from shape recognition to thinking about parts of a whole."
    },
    {
      "title": "Cake example",
      "quote": "پارٹ اس لیے لاتے ہیں اور ایکول پارٹ لاتے ہیں تاکہ اس میں لائن نہ آئے۔",
      "why": "You connected the math to a familiar real-life situation so students could see why equal parts matter."
    },
    {
      "title": "Notation check",
      "quote": "ٹھیک ہے، 9 ہم نے اوپر لکھنا ہے، اور 10؟",
      "why": "You caught a student’s mixed-up description and stepped in to clarify how numerator and denominator are placed."
    }
  ],
  "strength_name": "Warm engagement",
  "strength_note": "You kept students active with counting prompts, volunteer turns, and everyday language so many children answered and used fraction words.",
  "horizon_title": "two task levels",
  "horizon_note": "Offer one easier and one harder version of the practice question next time so students who finish early and those who struggle can work at different, productive levels.",
  "journey_note": "In this single lesson you showed steady, repeatable strengths that pushed the score to 71% — you keep showing up with the same strong routines.",
  "score_framing": "A 71% is a clear midpoint: it shows real strengths to build on and a focused next step that will make a big difference.",
  "domain_whys": {
    "high_leverage_practices": "This is developing because you used open prompts like 'کتنے پارٹس بنیں گے آپ کے حساب سے؟' and named procedures such as 'نمبریٹر' — it's not full marks because there was no targeted follow-up to students' answers to make their reasoning explicit.",
    "student_engagement": "This is strong because many students volunteered, counted aloud, and connected the idea to everyday life (for example, talking about sharing cake) — it's not full marks because interaction often stayed procedural and students asked task clarifications (for example, 'میم، میں کروں؟') rather than posing content questions about why the part–whole relationship holds.",
    "teacher_subject_knowledge": "This is developing because you correctly named numerator and denominator and identified proper fractions in the moment — it's not full marks because the lesson did not include any non-routine, multi-step problem or visible think time to deepen conceptual reasoning."
  }
}
